#!/bin/bash
# Pre-commit DISPATCHER. It runs checks; it implements none. Keep it that way.
#
# ENABLE PER CLONE (the hook is inert without this, exactly like .git-blame-ignore-revs):
#     git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# WHY: findings_row_lint.py, whose_row.py and merge_guard.sh all existed and none ran unless a lane
# remembered to run it -- the convention-that-requires-remembering problem one level down.
#
# THE ADMITTING RULE, which this header did not state until 2026-08-13 and should have:
#     a check belongs here iff a committer who did nothing wrong can always make it pass.
# Lane D's formulation (OI-64). It admits whole-tree invariants and still forbids gating on, say, a
# dirty unwaived tree. An earlier lane-A proposal -- "iff it can only fail on what THIS commit
# changed" -- was WRONG and is recorded because it is the plausible one: it generalised from the two
# exclusions below to *scope*, when their actual reasons are inescapability and wrong phase. Neither
# is about scope, and that rule forbids whole-tree invariants by construction.
#
# NOT RUN HERE, deliberately: generate_live_state.py --check-freshness, which returns 1 whenever
# LIVE-STATE.md's sha is not HEAD's -- a condition it CANNOT ESCAPE (BEN-191/BEN-199). A check that
# always fires is a check nobody reads, and putting it in a hook would train every lane to --no-verify.
# merge_guard.sh is also absent: it needs a lane argument and belongs at merge time, not commit time.
#
# DECLINED FOR NOW, WITH A NAMED UNLOCK TRIGGER: nd-unfolding/pet/check_canonical_designation.py.
# Added to this header 2026-08-14 by lane B because it was in NEITHER list -- the exact condition the
# paragraph below was written about after verify_hash_bindings.py, second instance, and this time in
# the namespace that comment came from (BEN-244). The defect was the silence; declaring it closes that.
#
#   WHAT IT IS. The fail-closed postcondition for the canonical-nominal DESIGNATION: promotion here
#   moves no bytes, so the whole safety argument is that the reference inventory be COMPLETE. On
#   substance it is exactly verify_hash_bindings.py's shape -- a whole-tree freeze invariant, unscoped
#   on purpose -- and it PASSED at exit=0 on 2026-08-11 (VERDICTS-20260811-session-D.md:452).
#
#   WHY IT IS NOT IN THE RUN LIST TODAY, measured at 849b70f rather than recalled: EXIT 1 -- 8
#   unaccounted files, 1 COUNT DRIFT (state/annealed-nominal-complete-56563761.json, expected 1 found 2
#   at lines 63/226), 2 stale inventory entries. It went red at the 2026-08-13 designation and the two
#   records of that designation are themselves among the occurrences it cannot account for. So a
#   committer who did nothing wrong CANNOT make it pass, which fails this dispatcher's admitting rule
#   at line 11 -- not because the check is wrong, but because the tree is.
#
#   AND IT CANNOT BE WIRED AS AN ADVISORY. `run()` discards a passing check's output, so a hook has
#   exactly two channels, silence and failure (BEN-226). "Wire it but only warn" does not exist here.
#
#   THE RED IS LEFT FOR ITS OWNER, DELIBERATELY. Making a check pass by editing its input is worse
#   than not having the check: it converts a real finding into a green tick. The inventory is the PET
#   lane's (the script is Session C's).
#
#   UNLOCK TRIGGER, single and checkable: when `python3 nd-unfolding/pet/check_canonical_designation.py`
#   exits 0 on a clean tree, move this to the run list as check 7. No other condition, no judgement
#   call -- run the command.
#
#   *** THE TRIGGER FIRED ON 2026-08-17 AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT HONOURED. DO NOT WIRE IT. ***
#   Recorded HERE, beside the trigger, so the next reader sees a DECISION rather than assuming the
#   trigger was missed -- which is this header's own rule about declining a check, applied to a
#   trigger. Ruling by the hook owner (mediator session, 2026-08-17); measured by lane E under
#   OI-96; full record in `BEN-387` and `docs/OPEN_ITEMS.md`'s OI-96 row.
#
#     MEASURED: `python3 nd-unfolding/pet/check_canonical_designation.py` -> EXIT 0 on a clean
#     tree. The trigger's stated condition is met, exactly and without interpretation.
#
#     AND THE CHECK STILL MUST NOT BE WIRED, because the trigger tests the wrong property. Its
#     enforcement is a WHOLE-FILE occurrence count of the namespace, and mutating the receipt
#     shows that count is INVERTED, not merely crude:
#       repoint `annealed-nominal-complete-56563761.json`'s canonical_baseline path to a SIBLING
#       inside the same protected directory -> count unchanged at 2, guard SILENT. That is the
#       BEN-133 repoint class the guard exists for.
#       delete the prose sentence at :245 -> count 2 -> 1, guard RED. A documentation tidy.
#     So wiring it installs a check that reddens on an innocent edit: a committer who did nothing
#     wrong CANNOT always make it pass, which fails the admitting rule at line 11 -- eleven lines
#     above the trigger that would have installed it. It would train lanes to `--no-verify`, which
#     is the cost this dispatcher's two other exclusions exist to avoid.
#
#     THE GENERAL FORM, which is why this block is long: A TRIGGER WRITTEN TO REMOVE JUDGEMENT IS
#     PRECISELY THE ONE PLACE NOBODY RE-DERIVES THE PROPERTY. Automating a decision does not
#     verify the predicate it automates -- it guarantees the predicate stops being checked. "Green
#     today" and "a committer who did nothing wrong can always make it pass" are different claims,
#     and the trigger asserts only the first.
#
#     WHAT WOULD ACTUALLY UNLOCK IT: replace the occurrence count with a field-level check, then
#     re-derive the admitting rule against the replacement. The field-level half now exists for
#     the protected namespace -- 44 declared pins in check 6, see this file's `verify_hash_bindings`
#     line and `state/regen_canonical_namespace_field_pins.py` -- so the remaining question is
#     whether anything in `check_canonical_designation.py` is still worth wiring once the pins
#     cover the repoint class. That is a judgement call, which is the point.
#
# ADMITTED TO THE RUN LIST 2026-08-14: docs/orchestration/verify_receipt_artifacts.py, as check 7.
# It was in NEITHER list -- the third instance of the exact silence this header's last paragraph is
# about (verify_hash_bindings.py, then check_canonical_designation.py above, now this).
#
#   MEASURED BEFORE DECIDING, not recalled: `python3 docs/orchestration/verify_receipt_artifacts.py`
#   EXIT 0 on a clean tree at 701b6c9 -- "203 receipts scanned at working tree, 3 deliverable-area
#   artifact path(s), 0 missing", positive control fires. Green on a tree nobody edited to make it
#   green, so it clears the admitting rule at line 11. Same shape as verify_hash_bindings.py: a
#   whole-tree invariant, unscoped on purpose.
#
#   WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, and this matters because it was wired in the same turn that repaired a
#   defect it CANNOT SEE. Nine .out files named by state/gate5-cstat-spec-measurements-20260814.json
#   were dropped by `.gitignore:13` (`*.out`) and `git add` no-opped silently; this check stayed green
#   throughout, for two independent reasons, both measured:
#     (a) `.out` is not in its EXT tuple (:39) -- the extension list is .npz/.npy/.h5/.hdf5/.root/
#         .pkl/.parquet, so the paths are never even extracted; and
#     (b) that receipt's `evidence` block keys are BARE FILENAMES ("centring_n50.out"), while
#         named_artifacts() (:66) requires a path starting with docs/orchestration/state/. So even
#         with .out added to EXT, this receipt's citations would still not be seen.
#   Its own --historical output says the same thing from the other end: case 2 (849b70f^) "does NOT
#   fire" because that needle is named by a METHOD-DECLARATION *.md, and scan() (:77,:83) reads only
#   state/*.json. NOT FIXED HERE ON PURPOSE -- widening EXT or the path rule is a scope change to
#   someone else's check, and the script's docstring (:19-23) argues the narrowness deliberately
#   (349 of 351 named paths are cluster products). Filed, not silently patched.
#
#   So: this check is admitted because it is green and real, NOT because it closes the *.out hole. It
#   does not. A future lane reading "7 checks passed" should not infer that a receipt's .out evidence
#   is tracked -- verify that with `git ls-files`.
#
# (Numbering note: the canonical-designation block above says "move this to the run list as check 7";
# that slot was taken, and slots 8 and 9 are now taken too, so its unlock makes it check 10. Its
# decision is untouched -- OI-81.)
#
# CHECKS 8 AND 9, "LIVE doc indexed" + its self-test, added 2026-08-17 by lane A at the mediator's
# request. Two run lines rather than one, following the `whose_row self-test` precedent already in the
# list: a guard whose self-test is not itself on a path anything takes is the class this hook exists to
# fix, one level down.
#
#   WHY: `RECONCILIATION-20260817-gbdtfive-macros-vs-rebuilt-candidate.md` was committed LIVE and this
#   hook printed "7 checks passed" while the document was in NO INDEX AT ALL. The pointer was added
#   because a peer asked, not because anything caught it. CLAUDE.md records nine findings that sat
#   orphaned exactly that way, and CONVENTION-document-retention.md:36 already required the
#   declaration -- what was missing was the check. Same shape as KNOWN_ISSUES 48: green on precisely
#   the case it does not cover.
#
#   IT SATISFIES THE ADMITTING RULE ABOVE -- "a committer who did nothing wrong can always make it
#   pass" -- and that is why it is SCOPED TO WHAT THIS COMMIT NEWLY DECLARES LIVE rather than
#   whole-tree. Measured before writing: 2 of the currently-LIVE .md docs are absent from CATALOG.md
#   (CONVENTION-document-retention.md, SPEC-20260814-gate5-cstat-construction-v1.md). A whole-tree
#   form would fail every lane's next commit on debt none of them created, which is how a check gets
#   routed around. The backlog is PRINTED on every run instead of hidden, and widening to whole-tree
#   is one line once those two are indexed -- deliberately somebody's decision, not the script's.
#
#   Note this is the narrow-scope form the header above records lane A as having proposed WRONGLY as a
#   general admitting rule. It is right *here* for a different reason: not "only fail on what this
#   commit changed" as a principle, but because the whole-tree variant fails on pre-existing debt and
#   therefore violates the real rule. The scope is a consequence of the admitting rule, not a rival to it.
#
#   COST: 0.07 s measured. Two small file reads and two `git show`s; it does NOT run
#   generate_manifest.py, which would be far too expensive for a hook and is itself ~140 lines stale.
#
#   WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it cannot detect a document that SHOULD be LIVE but was never declared. With
#   no overrides row the generator defaults to ARCHIVAL, so an undeclared doc is out of scope and this
#   check is silent. It enforces agreement between the author's declaration and the router -- not the
#   correctness of the declaration. Do not read this hook's all-passed line as "every live document is
#   findable" -- and note that the line is deliberately not quoted with its count here, per BEN-163:
#   prose restating a number the script prints is the copy that goes stale.
#
# THIS LIST IS THE ROOT CAUSE OF OI-64 AND IS NOW EXHAUSTIVE BY CONTRACT. Documenting exactly two
# exclusions made "never considered" indistinguishable from "considered and rejected":
# verify_hash_bindings.py was in neither list, so a Gate-4 code binding stayed broken ~18 h across four
# lanes' commits, every one of which printed "pre-commit: N checks passed". If you decline a check, say
# so HERE -- an unlisted check is not a decision, and the whole point of the omission being cheap to
# fix is that nobody could tell it had happened.
set -uo pipefail
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || exit 2

fail=0
# COUNTERS ADDED 2026-08-17. The all-passed line used to end `echo "pre-commit: 9 checks passed"` with
# the 9 HARDCODED -- the hook never counted its own checks. That is `BEN-163` inside the instrument the
# whole campaign reads as proof it ran: add a tenth check and the line still says 9; delete one and it
# still says 9. The literal happened to be correct (9 `run` lines) which is exactly why nobody noticed.
# Now derived, and the derived number is what the commit-msg trailer records.
ran=0
passed=0
run() {
  local n="$1"; shift
  ran=$((ran + 1))
  if "$@" >/tmp/.hook.$$ 2>&1; then
    passed=$((passed + 1))
  else
    echo "PRE-COMMIT FAIL: $n"; cat /tmp/.hook.$$; fail=1
  fi
}

run "findings long-form pointers" python3 docs/orchestration/findings_row_lint.py --longform
run "ledger VL ids"               python3 docs/orchestration/whose_row.py --check-ledger-ids
run "row-owner side table"        python3 docs/orchestration/whose_row.py --check-owners
run "whose_row self-test"         python3 docs/orchestration/whose_row.py --self-test
# OI-* has no block table and no addressing convention (OI-62(b), Joseph's call), so max(existing)+1 is
# the only available algorithm and two concurrent lanes collide BY CONSTRUCTION -- A and C both took
# OI-64 AND OI-65 on 2026-08-13, hours after A filed the warning (BEN-223). Writing the rule down did
# not work. This does not PREVENT a collision -- nothing local can, since the other lane's row is not in
# your tree until you pull -- it makes it loud at your next commit instead of at a rebase days later.
# Two existing duplicates are WAIVED IN THE SOURCE with reasons (whose_row.OI_ID_WAIVERS); they are
# annotated rather than renumbered because both ids are cited in pushed commits.
run "OPEN_ITEMS OI ids"           python3 docs/orchestration/whose_row.py --check-oi-ids
# WHOLE-TREE AND UNSCOPED, on purpose. Added 2026-08-13 (OI-64; Joseph authorized, lane D reviewed,
# receipt: AUTHORIZATION-20260813-hash-binding-gate-in-precommit.md). It guards every gate's code
# freeze, it is the reason the 2026-07-28 de-rooting refactor's six broken bindings were findable, and
# it was on no path anything took. 0.563 s measured, so scoping it to the staged diff would buy only
# blast-radius containment -- and for a freeze gate, containing the blast radius is a synonym for
# letting the break persist. IF THIS FAILS ON A RECEIPT YOU DID NOT TOUCH, that is the gate working:
# a broken freeze is the tree's problem, not its author's. Do not --no-verify past it and do not edit a
# digest to make it green -- re-issue or retire the owning receipt (files -> files_at_issue,
# sha256 -> sha256_at_issue, every digest preserved verbatim).
run "receipt+shell hash bindings"  python3 docs/orchestration/verify_hash_bindings.py
# Admitted 2026-08-14; rationale and MEASURED COVERAGE GAP in the header block above. Green at exit 0
# does NOT mean every receipt-cited artifact is tracked -- it means no *deliverable-area* .npz/.h5/etc
# path named by a state/*.json receipt is missing. .out/.err/.log are outside it.
run "receipt artifact tracking"    python3 docs/orchestration/verify_receipt_artifacts.py
# Added 2026-08-17; rationale, measured scope and stated LIMITS in the header block above. Its own
# --self-test is power-tested in BOTH directions (9 cases) and was additionally verified end-to-end
# against real `git add` staging in a throwaway worktree, because CONVENTION-lane-worktrees.md records
# that merge_guard.sh's one false pass was caught end-to-end and NOT by its self-test.
run "LIVE doc indexed"             python3 docs/orchestration/live_doc_indexed.py --check
run "LIVE-index self-test"         python3 docs/orchestration/live_doc_indexed.py --self-test

rm -f /tmp/.hook.$$
if [ "$fail" -eq 0 ]; then
  echo "pre-commit: ${passed} checks passed"
  # ONE-SHOT TOKEN for the commit-msg hook, which records the count as a commit trailer so that a hook
  # that never ran leaves a DURABLE absence instead of a missing terminal line. Written only on a clean
  # pass, deleted by commit-msg on use, so it cannot be inherited by a later commit. `--absolute-git-dir`
  # because a linked worktree's git dir is not `.git`.
  printf '%s\n' "$passed" > "$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir)/PRECOMMIT_CHECKS_PASSED" 2>/dev/null || true
fi
exit "$fail"
